    "body": "SolarEdge Technologies has launched the company’s Duo Power Optimizer for commercial and industrial (C&I) solar installations in the U.S. The company made the announcement November 16 ahead of the RE+ renewable energy conference in Las Vegas, Nevada.\nThe company said the Duo “delivers the same module-level energy harvest as a one-to-one power optimizer with half as many units to specify, wire and install, connecting two modules to one optimizer with an independent maximum power point tracker (MPPT) for each.” The group said the technology “also features SolarEdge’s innovative Arc Fault Detection, a global industry first that uses every optimizer as an arc sensor to detect arcs at the source rather than only at the inverter.”\nSolarEdge said that in a traditional string inverter system, the weakest module drags the output of the entire string, and the gap widens as modules degrade at different rates. With optimizers, an underperforming module affects only its own output.\n“The bottom line is true module-level energy harvesting and visibility, with fewer optimizers to buy, wire and install,” said Gokul Krishnan, SVP and general manager, North America at SolarEdge. “We also added safety features no one else in the industry has, including arc fault detection at the optimizer level. That addresses blind spots in the array where traditional string inverters can’t detect an arc. And the way we’ve designed the inputs gives installers real improvements in wire management on site.”\nSense Connect Technology\nSolarEdge said the Duo extends Sense Connect technology to every input and output connector, monitoring temperature to flag developing faults before an overheating connector can lead to an arc. The company said that “with every Power Optimizer acting as an individual arc sensor, detection is distributed across the array instead of relying on a single point at the inverter, so if one optimizer misses an arc, its neighbor can catch it. Detecting close to where an arc can form enhances accuracy and sensitivity, helping stop an arc before it escalates. Protection covers strings with up to 3,280 feet (1,000 meters) of total cable length between the inverter and optimizer, and issues are pinpointed down to the optimizer area for quick, cost-effective resolution.”\nEach optimizer has two input pairs: a short pair for the closest module, and a pre-extended pair for the adjacent one, reducing wire slack and eliminating field-made jumpers. Because one optimizer can connect two modules facing different azimuth angles, the Duo suits dual-tilt racking.\nThe Duo, which is manufactured in Florida, is designed to meet U.S. Domestic Content and FEOC (Foreign Entity of Concern) requirements and is expected to ship to international markets in 2027.\n“We saw a clear difference in wire management on the roof, with fewer connections to make and no extra jumpers to run, which saved our crews real time during installation,” said Aaron Wilson, CEO of Solar One. “SolarEdge did an excellent job with the extension lengths, leaving very little excess wiring for our crews to manage. With the Duo, installers get module-level visibility with 50% fewer optimizers than one-to-one designs like the C651U. Being able to monitor performance down to the individual module gives us greater confidence in the system’s long-term output and makes troubleshooting far more precise when we do need to return to the site.”\nSolarEdge is showcasing the Duo at RE+ 2026 in Las Vegas, Nov. 17-19, with shipments expected to begin in January 2027.\n— Darrell Proctor is a senior editor for POWER.\nTagged in:\nDuo Power Optimizer\nRE+\nsolar\nSolarEdge",
